WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.051
Authorization tests commonly prove that an
approved user succeeds and a disallowed

2
00:00:05.131 --> 00:00:06.349
user receives denial.

3
00:00:06.429 --> 00:00:08.800
Fail-secure assurance adds a third family:

4
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.573
the system cannot obtain a trustworthy
decision.

5
00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:15.370
Exercise missing identity and tenant
context, malformed role data,

6
00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.911
object lookup failure, database
exceptions, policy timeouts,

7
00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:22.581
invalid response shapes, unknown decision
values, cache misses,

8
00:00:22.661 --> 00:00:26.250
expired entries, circuit-open behavior,
and thrown exceptions.

9
00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.728
For each case, assert the absence of the
protected side effect.

10
00:00:29.808 --> 00:00:33.910
An HTTP status alone is insufficient if a
mutation occurred before the error

11
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:34.567
surfaced.

12
00:00:34.647 --> 00:00:38.621
Verify database state, emitted messages,
file changes, audit commands,

13
00:00:38.701 --> 00:00:40.944
and calls to privileged downstream APIs.

14
00:00:41.024 --> 00:00:45.511
For asynchronous flows, confirm background
work was not queued and retries cannot

15
00:00:45.591 --> 00:00:47.899
execute with lost authorization context.

16
00:00:47.979 --> 00:00:51.248
Use controllable fakes for clocks, policy
clients, caches,

17
00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:54.597
and repositories so failure timing remains
deterministic.

18
00:00:54.677 --> 00:00:59.099
Also test cancellation and late policy
completion so an abandoned request cannot

19
00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:01.038
resume into the protected branch.

20
00:01:01.118 --> 00:01:03.426
Review code from the side effect backward.

21
00:01:03.506 --> 00:01:05.877
Identify the exact branch that performs
it,

22
00:01:05.957 --> 00:01:08.393
the value that exists before
authorization,

23
00:01:08.473 --> 00:01:11.806
and every exception or return path that
can reach that branch.

24
00:01:11.886 --> 00:01:14.514
Look for undefined or null interpreted as
allow,

25
00:01:14.594 --> 00:01:18.376
permissive fallback constants, unawaited
checks, missing returns,

26
00:01:18.456 --> 00:01:22.750
stale permits without limits, and
secondary entry points that omit the policy.

27
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.381
Test new enum values and malformed remote
data so TypeScript assumptions do not mask

28
00:01:27.461 --> 00:01:28.679
runtime uncertainty.

29
00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:31.323
Observe denial and indeterminate
separately.

30
00:01:31.403 --> 00:01:35.698
Metrics for policy latency, timeouts,
validation failures, cache freshness,

31
00:01:35.778 --> 00:01:40.393
and dependency health support availability
work without weakening authorization.

32
00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:45.152
Security logs should capture subject and
resource references appropriate to the risk

33
00:01:45.232 --> 00:01:48.052
while avoiding tokens and unnecessary
policy data.

34
00:01:48.132 --> 00:01:52.363
Alert on sustained evaluation failures and
unexpected use of stale evidence.

35
00:01:52.443 --> 00:01:56.288
The operational response is to restore
trustworthy decision-making,

36
00:01:56.368 --> 00:01:58.740
never to make access the emergency
default.
